      "full_text": "Esparza, 44, is accused of hiring a hitman to kill Ramirez in 2004, a decade after the alleged rape occurred. She has maintained her innocence, claiming that she had been trying to find Ramirez to report the rape to police, but had lost track of him until she received a letter from him in 2003 asking for \"I want to find justice for Gonzalo Ramirez, but not at the expense of my own innocence,\" Esparza said, her voice trembling with \"I want to stand trial. I want my day in court. I want to tell my story.\" Esparza's attorneys argue that the prosecution's case is and that there is no physical evidence linking Esparza to the murder. They also claim that the prosecution has withheld exculpatory evidence, including a letter Ramirez wrote to Esparza in 2003 in which he admitted to raping her and apologized. \"We believe that the prosecution is attempting to railroad my client into a plea bargain because they know they don't have a strong case,\" Tarlow said. \"We are that if this case goes to trial, a jury will acquit Ms. Esparza.\" Orange County District Attorney Todd Spitzer's office declined to comment on the specifics of the case, citing a gag order issued by the judge. However, Spitzer has previously stated that Esparza's claims of innocence are smoke screen to hide her guilt.\" Esparza's preliminary hearing is scheduled to begin on Monday. If the judge rules that there is enough",
